WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:01.810
We have a voicemail number.

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:04.560
That number is two one nine two David P..

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.610
Here's just a little break from from the depressing
politics.

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:12.240
There's a really funny debate going on in
our audience right now about whether I am

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:18.070
providing too much of a setup for the video
and audio clips that I play on the show.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:21.160
Here's a caller who thinks I should be providing
more of a setup.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:22.160
Take a listen to this.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:23.160
Hi, David.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.730
I'm calling in.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:33.250
Heavy support of your usual format of preloading
as before you play a clip.

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:36.110
In normal presenting of information, I love
the idea of getting straight to the content

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:38.739
and then analyzing it after.

00:00:38.739 --> 00:00:41.010
And I respect those who who have requested
that.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:47.861
However, when in the case stuff coming out
of the Republican administration would trump

00:00:47.861 --> 00:00:48.861
himself.

00:00:48.861 --> 00:00:56.910
It is so full of spin and desolating, which
is kind of abusive, that preloading us with

00:00:56.910 --> 00:01:00.870
information for me really helps relieve the
pain.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:01.870
Of.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:05.210
I'm happy to hear it.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:13.840
And also, it is a vaccination against the
upcoming sprint.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.779
Makes me much more immune to it.

00:01:16.779 --> 00:01:17.859
I did, actually.

00:01:17.859 --> 00:01:22.779
So I'm a strong supporter of the employee
front loading.

00:01:22.779 --> 00:01:32.259
OK, so my idea for years has been I'm not
like I'm not a video playback technician.

00:01:32.259 --> 00:01:35.520
The idea here is I'm giving you my take.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.960
And so I try to prepare people for what we're
going to see.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:44.279
And I certainly give more of a lead up to
clips than some other hosts might.

00:01:44.279 --> 00:01:45.279
And it was deliberate.

00:01:45.279 --> 00:01:47.240
I was doing that on purpose.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:50.920
And every once in a while, I would get e-mails
from people saying, David, just give us the

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:55.869
clip and then analyze it so that you're not
tainting our view and and sort of priming

00:01:55.869 --> 00:01:58.130
us to believe one thing or the other.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:01:59.340
Then after tell us what you think.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:02.079
And by that time we'll have made it made our
own sort of judgment of it.

00:02:02.079 --> 00:02:04.549
And I tried that yesterday and I've tried
it a little more recently.

00:02:04.549 --> 00:02:09.210
And I have to tell you, overwhelmingly, overwhelmingly,
the reaction was, no, no, David, don't just

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:10.800
play the clips, don't you?

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.930
Don't be a video playback clerk, essentially.

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:16.330
We are here for you to give us context.

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:18.810
And the context before is actually important.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.400
So this is not a huge deal.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:24.250
It's an interesting difference of opinion
among the audience.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:25.880
But point taken.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:30.980
And the response overwhelmingly in the last
24 to 48 hours is please go back to the old

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:31.980
way.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:32.980
Tell us what we're about to see.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:37.200
And we can still say we disagree with you,
but we like to see what is your take on what

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.200
we're about to see.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.400
So I'll probably go back in that direction.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.720
We have a great bonus show for you today.

